replaceMexicoSection("mexico","position","<p>Mexico rejects formal bloc membership and foreign military bases. American geography and the Monroe ceiling constrain security policy, but domestic politics, resource ownership, currency, culture, and corporate regulation remain explicitly Mexican.</p>");
replaceMexicoSection("mexico","development","<p>The <a href='institutional-revolutionary-party-mexico.html'>PRI state</a> inherits the Revolution, the <a href='mexican-miracle.html'>Mexican Miracle</a>, and a truce that permits worship while excluding independent Catholic public power. <a href='mexican-catholic-civil-society.html'>Catholic civil society</a> grows through schools, cooperatives, professions, municipal politics, <a href='national-action-party-mexico.html'>PAN legalism</a>, and <a href='national-synarchist-union.html'>Sinarquista</a> networks until federal repression produces regional war.</p>");
replaceMexicoSection("mexico","economy","<p><a href='pemex.html'>Pemex</a>, oil, mining, agriculture, protected manufacturing, border industry, railways, and ports support a large national market. Procurement favors capable Mexican firms, then Latin suppliers as counterweights, while accepting the American investment and trade geography makes impossible to exclude.</p>");
replaceMexicoSection("mexico","security","<p>The postwar state integrates loyal armed forces, regional police, and selected former Catholic-national formations under an explicit ban on permanent foreign bases. Washington accepts Mexican autonomy while retaining the ability to close the border, ports, finance, and strategic supply if rival military systems appear.</p>");
replaceMexicoSection("mexico","cristiada","<p>The <a href='second-cristiada.html'>Second Cristiada</a> grows from a disputed western election, repression of Catholic organization, and lethal religious confrontation. The <a href='national-catholic-coalition-mexico.html'>National Catholic coalition</a> joins civil institutions, regional governments, local defense, and defecting officers. Buchanan and Vatican-backed mediation produce the 1979–80 settlement and consolidation through 1985.</p>");
appendMexicoSection("mexico",{id:"historical-foundations",title:"Historical foundations",html:"<p>The <a href='first-cristero-war.html'>1926–29 Cristiada</a> ends by negotiation without removing the anticlerical constitutional order. The <a href='cristero-movement.html'>Cristero tradition</a> survives through family and devotional memory, while the UNS and PAN develop militant and constitutional forms of Catholic opposition.</p>"});
appendMexicoSection("mexico",{id:"crown-question",title:"The Crown question",html:"<p>The <a href='mexican-crown-movement.html'>Mexican Crown movement</a> emerges only after the Catholic-national republic confronts presidential and military succession. Iturbide, new-dynasty, and elective-hereditary models remain debated; no sovereign or restoration is established.</p>"});
